{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GfFqghBNPNT5Zqz7Jz5YDK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QpxVSbe4waCPpAFyjHQHtD","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"ec_hte_llm_annotation","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3513,"cache_read_input_tokens":17567,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3513,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":43,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013t9D436on89EBGbspTz6id","session_id":"ca75923e-c9c6-4829-ae10-fa16ed68897c","uuid":"4773cb48-5e67-44b0-862b-54871f5f1697"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QpxVSbe4waCPpAFyjHQHtD","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\\\\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\\\\n- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\\\\n\\\\n1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \\\\\\\"debiasing\\\\\\\" can worsen CATE bias by 5-10x compared to no correction at all.\\\\n\\\\n2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) eliminates this bias while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. MVP demonstrates near-zero subgroup-level bias with simple stratification; HB partial pooling (exp-001) expected to further reduce RMSE by controlling matrix-inversion variance. Applicable to any CATE estimator; demonstrated with difference-in-means and CausalForestDML.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic: when this product exceeds a threshold, global correction is harmful and stratified correction is needed. This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE.\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis (e.g., \\\\\\\"tweet contains economic framing\\\\\\\" moderates campaign effectiveness). LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\\\\n\\\\n- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \\\\\\\"problem identification + principled solution\\\\\\\" not \\\\\\\"new estimation technique.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"On semi-synthetic political-science benchmarks, global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by up to 7.8x in low-error subgroups; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\\\\n\\\\n1. **Bias mechanism identification + formal bound**: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity. Includes \\\\\\\"debiasing can hurt\\\\\\\" proposition with constructive example. (Theory + Empirical)\\\\n2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\\\\n3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\\\\n4. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data case study (pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark) with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n1. **\\\\\\\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\\\\\\\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\\\\n\\\\n2. **\\\\\\\"How is this different from PPCI / DSL?\\\\\\\"** — PPCI corrects outcome-side measurement error via kernel localization; we correct covariate-side. Orthogonal, and composable. DSL applied per-subgroup is a valid baseline but lacks cross-subgroup shrinkage, operates at estimand level rather than measurement model level, and has strictly higher variance under partial pooling. Do NOT say \\\\\\\"DSL cannot do subgroup correction\\\\\\\" — say it lacks the principled shrinkage mechanism.\\\\n\\\\n3. **\\\\\\\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\\\\\\\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\\\\n\\\\n4. **\\\\\\\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\\\\\\\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\\\\n\\\\n5. **\\\\\\\"Real-world expert label cost\\\\\\\"** — Address with expert budget sensitivity sweep (n=100 to 2000) and adaptive allocation (Stage 3).\\\\n\\\\n6. **\\\\\\\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\\\\\\\"** — Carroll et al. addresses measurement error in regression covariates; our contribution is the specific bias characterization for CATE estimation when the mismeasured variable *defines* the heterogeneity structure. The Δπ×Δτ product form is a new result in this context. Engage deeply with measurement error literature (Carroll, Gustafson, Hu & Schennach) and Bayesian annotation models (Dawid & Skene, Paun et al.) to contextualize precisely.\\\\n\\\\n7. **\\\\\\\"Methodological novelty is modest\\\\\\\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. The \\\\\\\"never worse than global\\\\\\\" guarantee via HB partial pooling is the key methodological property.\\\\n\\\\n8. **\\\\\\\"Why no figures?\\\\\\\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\n\\\\nEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use case (political messaging, refugee interviews), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track.\\\\n\\\\nRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 7657, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013t9D436on89EBGbspTz6id","session_id":"ca75923e-c9c6-4829-ae10-fa16ed68897c","uuid":"4179cf1d-833c-476f-af37-88d55621465e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:27:30.712Z"}
